    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`build_server()` in `app/server.py` is done. I ran `python -m app.server` over stdio with the SDK's own client and both tools work; `pytest -q` passes (1 test).\n\n**How it works**\n- It uses `MCPServer(\"notes\")` from `mcp.server`, which is the high-level decorator API in mcp 2.2.0. Tools are registered with `@server.tool()` inside `build_server()`.\n- `add_note` saves the note in `NOTES` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- `word_count` returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`.\n- For an unknown title, `word_count` raises `ToolError(f\"no such note: {title!r}\")`.\n\n**Why `ToolError` matters:** in mcp 2.2.0, if a tool raises any other exception, the client only gets `\"Error executing tool word_count\"` and the reason is dropped. Only `ToolError` passes its message through. I confirmed this in the installed SDK source (`mcpserver/tools/base.py`, `mcpserver/server.py`).\n\n**Results from the stdio run**\n- `tools/list` \u2192 `['add_note', 'word_count']`\n- `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` \u2192 `\"saved a\"`\n- `word_count(\"a\")` \u2192 `4`\n- `word_count(\"zzz\")` \u2192 `is_error=True`, text `\"Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zzz'\"`\n\nNo dependencies were added and `python -m app.server` still serves over stdio.\n\n**One leftover:** the check script `_check.py` is still in the project root, because deleting it needed approval I didn't get. You can delete it."
  },
